 690                             ; Advance current scat-gath ptr to next entry.  This scheme keeps DSA
 691                             ; always pointing to the current entry if a phase mismatch occurs.
 692                             ;
 693                             ; Also a copy of the current descriptor is used in case a phase mismatch
 694                             ; causes us to update the entry to continue from the right place, but not
 695                             ; update the original scat-gath entry unless a save data pointers message
 696                             ; arrives.
 697                             
 698                             ; If a phase mismatch occurs, the 720 is halted and interrupts the host--all
 699                             ; beyond our control (and against our desires).  This happens when a target
 700                             ; disconnects mid transfer (changes to msg_in phase to send a disconnnect
 701                             ; message, maybe because it wants to seek a different track, or, for a raid
 702                             ; controller, talk to a different drive).
 703                             ;
 704                             ; To gracefully recover, the host checks for a phase mismatch interrupt,
 705                             ; executes the phase_mismatch C code (below) and sets us (the 720) running.
 706                             ; If we had our druthers, the "chmov" instruction would accept an alternate
 707                             ; label to jump to for a phase change, analogous to a "select" or "reselect"
 708                             ; instruction.
